comment 337074301: Vox Day 2026-09-14T18:11:27.590Z edited_at=None
  OK   In any population over 10,000, neutral substitution will not fixate at all.
  OK   This is Kimura's standard neutral theory equation for how long it takes a neutral mutation to fixate
  OK   With the standard human effective population size, Nₑ = 10,000:
  OK   t̄ = 4 × 10,000 = 40,000 generations. At 25 years per generation, that's 1,000,000 years per neutral fixation.
  OK   Now, it's actually a lot worse for the neutral substitution case than that, because there is a hard limit to genetic drift even being able to affect a population. In any population over 10,000, neutral substitution will not fixate at all. And we have actually found empirical evidence for this mathematical barrier in the ancient DNA database.
